                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b28a3de2-ba18-3661-8f82-1613a48920e2",
                        "entry_id": "433c9c17-6d47-3c9b-a83f-a0c1db813c75",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To bring out; deliver; utter; express."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3b6e79ff-3b2a-4fda-813c-6c0a4f93a41c",
                                "sense_id": "b28a3de2-ba18-3661-8f82-1613a48920e2",
                                "text": "We utter or outbring acts; the word of God produced creatures."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02899ed7-2c8c-459f-aba1-18619b1da4e3",
                                "sense_id": "b28a3de2-ba18-3661-8f82-1613a48920e2",
                                "text": "All these, with many more, she deftly wrought Into gay wreaths and posies passing fair; Then from the inner sacristy outbrought Vases of simplest clay, but shapes most rare, And round the statue's base, as quick as thought, [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "75ed4a6a-e786-48c0-96ae-b14d10e61dd6",
                                "sense_id": "b28a3de2-ba18-3661-8f82-1613a48920e2",
                                "text": "Whose breath of mouth divine, Of old from the deep gulf outbrought me, And who all beautiful outwrought me."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d022eabd-3f56-465d-8179-c04aa4ef338c",
                                "sense_id": "b28a3de2-ba18-3661-8f82-1613a48920e2",
                                "text": "And then Sir Launcelot said: Now have good day, my lord the king, for wit you well ye win no worship at these walls; and if I would my knights outbring, there should many a man die."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "da1074bf-0d21-46d2-ac0d-e6dd400ca709",
                                "sense_id": "b28a3de2-ba18-3661-8f82-1613a48920e2",
                                "text": "Tea, seeds of crescive sympathy Were sown by those more excellent than he, Long known, though long contemned till then — The gods of men in amity. Souls have grown seers, and thought outbrings The mournful many-sidedness of things With foes as friends, enfeebling ires And fury-fires by gaingiving!"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
